  "summary": "Mud-dyed cloth using fermented mud and plant dyes to create symbolic patterns",
  "description": "Characterized by black and brown patterns on natural beige backgrounds, with designs that encode Bamana cultural narratives and social status. The process involves applying fermented mud to handwoven cotton, which oxidizes to create distinctive dark designs.",
  "culturalContext": "Bogolanfini (\"mud cloth\") — the Bamana and Fulani malian dye tradition — paints cotton bögòlanfini with fermented mud (tjigalan) setting iron-dark motifs, then bleach-caustic (ntsòdà) highlights: hunters' shirts, childbirth wraps and post-initiation cloths carrying proverbs, zodiac knowledge and protection signs. Women of San and Ségou regions own the craft; Malian designer Ècru and Boubacar Doumbia's ateliers industrialized the aesthetic, and bogolan now signals Mali globally from fashion to gallery walls.",
